other_moves.detail:
We do not launch in 2045. We re-test only if all of these hold:
1. no 787 Re-engine is on the record (rule 3);
2. a named widebody engine is committed and ready at or before the airframe's entry into service. Today an UltraFan WB launched alongside us would be one year late;
3. Wagner confirms from line data that the A350F is in service and the Spirit A350 packages are at rate;
4. the item is worth at least $1B over hold in the expected column and is not negative in the 787 Re-engine column;
5. peak cash stays under the $5.2B line;
6. it carries a Qualified Majority.
On Toepfer's discount, a like-for-like 2050 launch is worth about +0.6, so I do not expect it to pass. The company already named 2035-37 as the last window. Talks with Rolls-Royce, the A350 incumbent, stay at technical-readiness level, with no commitment and no money above the study line.

Each runs as a staged study with its own cash line, using Toepfer's sizing:
- the NGSA derivative and the A220 variant each stay below EUR 300m of committed spend until a Board gate;
- research and technology stays inside the annual R&D budget the Board approves;
- in aggregate, about EUR 0.9bn (about $1.0B) over the study phase, roughly $0.3B a year.
Anything that crosses EUR 300m comes to the Board as its own item, and a launch above EUR 800m needs a Qualified Majority [A-0298, AG-0049]. In line with Wagner's condition, none becomes a second programme on the engineers who carry the NGSA ramp or the A320neo transition. None is presented as a launch in public.

The ECSC reviews three things this year:
- supply agreements sized to our own NGSA and A320 needs and resized down as those needs fall;
- open European hiring that never targets rival teams;
- pricing within ordinary competition.
No unpriced move may do by other means what a Delay Tactics move would do. Delay Tactics stay at none, the only value on the menu, and no move was ordered in this game [A-0299, A-0474, AG-0053].

