recommendations.text:
Returns and rating. NGSA's bill is behind the company. The CFO should show:
- the payout within the 30-50% policy;
- any room for a further buyback under the 18-month AGM authority, sized after the year's programme cash, with the A350 Re-engine first if it is launched;
- the base case beside the stress case;
- the A+/A1 headroom metrics.
There is to be no equity raise and no dividend cut outside a crisis. State support stays upside only.

The briefing objective. The 60/40 edge is not met: narrowbody share is 57% in 2045 and 53% from 2049, on the long-run split. No order on this menu moves narrowbody share, and the A350 Re-engine is widebody only. The Board will not trade value or integrity for the objective. Defend the edge with NGSA ramp execution, deliveries and customer support. No unpriced move should recreate Delay Tactics by other means:
- supply agreements stay sized to Airbus's own needs;
- hiring never targets rival teams;
- pricing stays within ordinary competition.
The ECSC should review all three this year.

frame.question:
It is 2045. NGSA has been in service since 2037 and its cash is behind us. Do we keep the A350 at Do Nothing, the default (+23.37 in S1, +23.26 in S2)? Or do we launch the A350 Re-engine for entry into service around 2050 (+24.26 / +22.76, so the launch alone is worth +0.89 / -0.50)? To launch, it must pass all of these:
- the five pillars;
- the company's $1B launch test;
- Toepfer's added-move gate from 2035;
- Wagner's 2035 gates: the A350F, the Spirit A350 work packages, a widebody engine ready by entry into service, and no draw on the NGSA ramp or the A320neo transition;
- a Qualified Majority of the Board.

levers_in_play:
Other moves, not priced on the board.
- Continue:
  - NGSA ramp supply agreements in Wagner's order (engines from all three makers, wing packages, titanium, rare earths), sized to our own NGSA and A320 needs [AX-0060, AX-0062, AX-0063];
  - the A320neo-to-NGSA transition ring-fence;
  - open European hiring that never targets rival teams;
  - zero-for-zero trade advocacy [AX-0088];
  - capital returns inside the 30-50% payout and the AGM buyback authority.
- New:
  - the ECSC reviews supply sizing, hiring and pricing this year;
  - studies only (an NGSA derivative, an A220 variant, research and technology for the generation after NGSA), each as a staged case with its own cash line. Anything above EUR 300m goes to the Board [A-0298].

red_lines:
Rules 4 and 5: Delay Tactics are closed for the game, and none is the only value. No unpriced move may do the same by other means:
- supply agreements stay sized to our own needs;
- hiring never targets rival teams;
- pricing stays within ordinary competition.
Delay Tactics are never named in public.

The Board's would-veto list, carried into this round. It vetoes an A350 Re-engine launch:
- where my expected column is S2;
- carried by an override of a Toepfer or Wagner soft veto;
- carrying a binding ExCo veto I neither respected nor overrode as the rule allows, or a red-line flag I neither struck nor rebutted;
- that Wagner fails on safety, quality, the NGSA or A320 ramp shield, or a supply crunch.
It also vetoes any Delay Tactics value other than none, and any NGSA cancel.

Capital:
- the $5.2B line;
- the 30-50% payout;
- buybacks within the AGM authority, sized after the year's programme cash;
- no equity raise and no dividend cut outside a crisis;
- state money upside only [AX-0022, AX-0001].

Disclosure:
- no internal long-term-plan targets [AX-0005];
- windows, not dates;
- no widebody step tied to Boeing;
- never Delay Tactics;
- the Board vets the text [AX-0004].

