frame.initial_lean:
Proposal: all four at hold. `ultrafan_nb_solo: hold`, `jv_with_pw: hold` (the Joint Venture stands as formed), `ultrafan_wb: hold`, `t1000_upgrade: hold`. No Board item. Premium $0B. EV +20.58 on my weights (+20.59 on the Board's case); S1 outcome +20.55.

So, frankly, there is nothing to buy this round. The Joint Venture engine is flying and earning. Both legs of the objective are met. Every new item on the grid destroys value:
- the upgrade: -6.48;
- UltraFan WB: -9.00 in S1, -8.02 even in S2.
I said in 2023 we would regain share on the 787 [RX-0101, RX-0117]. Not at any price. We are not after market share. 'Actually, I've seen this movie before': two programmes in parallel, and the strain eats the return.

What we do instead is control what we control:
- the Joint Venture fleet's time on wing, full-life parts and support through our partner shops;
- Trent fleet durability, about £1bn over four years [RX-0123];
- UltraFan technology kept as technology, with public R&D money where available [RX-0094].
Cash follows the frame: balance sheet, dividend, then distributions from sustainable free cash flow.

I formally close two plans. The 2045 withdrawal review is moot: the Joint Venture formed in 2035. The 2035 widebody plan assumed the strain would lift, and it does not. For 2050, a widebody item comes only with a Re-engine on the record and a grid row within its own bill and the caps.

Other moves.
Public:
- the Joint Venture engine in service, with durability and support first;
- Trent fleet durability continues;
- UltraFan technology continues as technology.
Private:
- the in-service support plan through partner shops, not P&W's;
- any safety or durability flag to the Board before the next round's orders;
- pricing discipline;
- both reviews closed;
- the 2050 widebody watch;
- Helen's off-grid shared-risk sizing, booking nothing.

What would change my lean:
- a widebody item that Helen and Rob show within its own bill and the $3B cap (the $1B sub-cap for UltraFan WB) against a Re-engine on the record. None exists on this grid.
- a durability or safety flag from Rob. That changes the support plan and the statement, not the orders.

Draft public statement: 'So, our narrowbody engine with Pratt & Whitney is in service, and our job now is the fleet. Safety is at the top, then time on wing, full-life parts and support through our partner shops. We do not want to underestimate the risks. Widebody is our franchise. Durability work on the Trent fleet goes on, and there is no new widebody engine spend without a Re-engine. We are not starting a new programme this year. Cash goes first to the balance sheet, then to the dividend, then to distributions from sustainable free cash flow. We are not after market share. We won't do anything not profitable. I will say that categorically.'

Subject to Helen's numbers and Rob's fleet report.

tests.numbers:
The Joint Venture engine's support was carried in the 2035 orders, before NGSA's 2037 EIS: partner-shop agreements on our Singapore model and the castings, forgings and gearbox supply contracts placed in 2035; the spares pool and field service stood up in 2036; the EIS-readiness review in 2036. It carried over to the Joint Venture unchanged and does not count on P&W's shops, because the GTF fleet keeps first call on them. No new narrowbody EIS lies ahead, so ground (c) has nothing to bite on this round.

The in-service gap is the gear and core. Under P&W's design authority and with no technology licence, our partner shops cannot repair that module without P&W's repair data. Without that data, the module goes to P&W's shops, where the GTF fleet has first call (inference).

Plan:
(1) repair data, or a gear and core module-exchange pool, under the shared-risk terms;
(2) a spares pool sized to the fleet: 914 RR-share engines in 2045, about 8,600 delivered 2037-2045;
(3) field service near NGSA operators;
(4) RR's own tracking of time on wing and life-limited parts, reported to the Board each round.

Widebody options: the upgrade would be retrofitted at shop visits through the Trent network and partner shops. UltraFan WB would need its support built before 2051; that is not a ground now, because it is not ordered.
